  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Let $O$ be the origin and suppose points $A$ and $B$ lie on the graph of $y=x^2$. Given $\\triangle AOB$ is equilateral, find the side length of the triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$2\\sqrt{3}$\n\nUse symmetry to write $A=(t,t^2)$ and $B=(-t,t^2)$, then equate $OA$ and $AB$.",
      "worked": "Since $OA=OB$, their squared distances from the origin are equal. For a point $(x,x^2)$ this squared distance is $x^2+x^4$, which increases with $x^2\\geq0$. Thus $A$ and $B$ have equal absolute $x$-coordinates. They are distinct, so by symmetry write $A=(t,t^2)$ and $B=(-t,t^2)$, where $t>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}AB&=2t\\\\OA^2&=t^2+t^4\\\\OA^2&=AB^2\\\\t^2+t^4&=4t^2\\\\t^4&=3t^2\\\\t^2&=3\\\\AB&=2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
